  The reason the pure hard cut loses: [38;5;153m"The quick brown fox"[39m at [38;5;153mmaxLength: 14[39m gives [38;5;153m"The quick b..."[39m. Mid-word fragments
  are the thing truncation UI gets complained about. It also produces [38;5;153m"The quick ..."[39m at 13 — a trailing space wedged
  against the ellipsis — so the "simpler" version needs a [38;5;153mtrimEnd[39m anyway, and half its simplicity advantage evaporates.

  The reason the pure word boundary loses: a single long token ([38;5;153m"supercalifragilistic"[39m, a URL, a hash) has no space to
  break at, so it degrades to returning bare [38;5;153m"..."[39m — all information destroyed. It needs a fallback regardless.

  Combining them is about four lines more than the hard cut, and the fallback branch is exactly the case where
  word-boundary behavior is wrong. You get clean breaks on prose and graceful behavior on unbreakable tokens.

  [1mBehavior[0m

  - [38;5;153mtruncate: n[39m — if the result is longer than [38;5;153mn[39m, cut it so the returned string is at most [38;5;153mn[39m characters, ellipsis
    included.
  - Cut at the last space within the first [38;5;153mn - 3[39m characters; if there is no such space, cut at exactly [38;5;153mn - 3[39m.
  - [38;5;153mtrimEnd[39m before appending [38;5;153m'...'[39m, so no [38;5;153m"word ..."[39m.
  - Final [38;5;153m.slice(0, n)[39m as a backstop, which also handles [38;5;153mn < 3[39m (you get a clipped ellipsis rather than a string longer
    than the max you asked for).
  - Guarded by truthiness like every other option, so [38;5;153mtruncate: 0[39m is a no-op — consistent with the file's existing
    style, and the alternative ("truncate everything to empty") isn't useful.

